WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:07.628
Paper may not seem like a significant invention, but it is no exaggeration to say that it has changed the course of civilization.

2
00:00:07.629 --> 00:00:18.038
Not only is paper used to spread important knowledge and revolutionary ideas, but it also affects people's daily lives, the modern economy, and the environment.

3
00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:22.535
The word "paper" comes from "papyrus," the plant first used to make paper.

4
00:00:22.536 --> 00:00:32.450
In about 3000 BC, the ancient Egyptians found that laying strips of papyrus at right angles and then drying them would create thin sheets of cream-colored material.

5
00:00:32.451 --> 00:00:40.091
They recorded important events on these sheets. Soon, the ancient Greeks adopted this technology, as would the Romans after them.

6
00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:45.025
Paper made from papyrus, however, was difficult to make and expensive.

7
00:00:45.026 --> 00:00:56.760
During the third century BC, the Greeks also began to use bound sheets of parchment (a writing material made from stretched sheep or goat skin) or vellum (made from the skin of a calf) to write on.

8
00:00:56.761 --> 00:01:04.992
These early books had to be laboriously hand-copied, word by word. As a result, only the church and the very wealthy could afford them.

9
00:01:04.993 --> 00:01:11.825
Before paper and printing became widespread, very few people had access to information beyond what they were told at church.

10
00:01:11.826 --> 00:01:19.825
Most scholars agree that a Chinese official invented the method of papermaking that today's processes are based on in 105 AD.

11
00:01:19.826 --> 00:01:28.062
This technique involved soaking, washing, and then beating rags, tree bark, and other materials into pulp using a wooden mallet.

12
00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:33.744
The pulp was then laid out on wire mesh and dried to form thin sheets.

13
00:01:33.745 --> 00:01:37.653
Papermaking soon spread from China to other parts of the world.

14
00:01:37.654 --> 00:01:44.549
It was introduced to Korea during the third century and to India through traders traveling the Silk Road by the eighth century.

15
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.713
Paper eventually reached Europe in the twelfth century through Italian merchants who traded with Arabs.

16
00:01:49.714 --> 00:01:59.127
The knowledge of how to make this material became so valuable that skilled Chinese papermakers were sometimes even taken captive and forced to make paper for their captors.

17
00:01:59.128 --> 00:02:07.353
An important innovation occurred in the tenth century AD, when a Chinese man, Pi Sheng, invented the first system of movable type out of clay.

18
00:02:07.354 --> 00:02:13.770
Movable type was a major development because it meant that multiple copies of a book could be printed easily, cheaply, and quickly.

19
00:02:13.771 --> 00:02:20.349
Then around 1400 AD, King Taejong of Korea ordered the creation of the first metal movable type.

20
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:29.184
Unfortunately, neither of these inventions was widely used because the eighty thousand characters of the Chinese language made movable type rather impractical.

21
00:02:29.185 --> 00:02:38.502
Movable type finally gained widespread recognition and use when Johannes Gutenberg invented his version of it in Mainz, Germany, in 1450.

22
00:02:38.503 --> 00:02:44.262
By the end of that century, printing presses had been set up in more than 250 cities across Europe.

23
00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:49.824
With the use of the printing press, new ideas about culture, religion, and commerce spread quickly throughout Europe.

24
00:02:49.825 --> 00:02:56.156
When printing technology spread to Italy, the Renaissance (a flowering of art, architecture, and literature) was born.

25
00:02:56.157 --> 00:03:02.639
One of the most influential events of the time was the Reformation, the Protestant movement against the Catholic Church.

26
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:15.136
In 1517, a German religious scholar named Martin Luther started the Reformation when he posted what are now known as his "95 Theses" on the doors of Castle Church in Wittenburg.

27
00:03:15.137 --> 00:03:22.811
The 95 Theses opposed some of the Catholic Church's practices, most notably the selling of "indulgences," or pardons for sins.

28
00:03:22.812 --> 00:03:33.044
Others reprinted this document, and soon Luther's views attracted enough followers to weaken the Catholic Church. This led to the rise of Protestantism.

29
00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:40.615
Thus, printing made it possible for the Reformation to change the role of the church and the religious, social, and political face of Europe.

30
00:03:40.616 --> 00:03:46.616
In eighteenth-century England, another movement, called the Industrial Revolution, began to change people's lives.

31
00:03:46.617 --> 00:03:50.362
Before then, most people were farmers and lived in the countryside.

32
00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:58.512
During the Industrial Revolution, large sections of the rural population migrated into cities to get jobs in the many factories being built there.

33
00:03:58.513 --> 00:04:06.280
The Industrial Revolution also changed printing by mechanizing the process of making paper, setting type, printing, and binding.

34
00:04:06.281 --> 00:04:12.349
This meant that books could be mass-produced cheaply, and for the first time, everyone could afford to buy them.

35
00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:20.922
The prevalence of books meant that many people wanted to learn to read, so widespread public education became more common in the nineteenth century.

36
00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:26.163
As literacy increased, so did demand for magazines, collections of poetry, and novels.

37
00:04:26.164 --> 00:04:32.066
And as the range of available reading materials expanded, so did the modern concept of literature.

38
00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:39.735
Paper also made many contributions to the fine arts. It became a popular medium for watercolor paintings, sketches, and posters.

39
00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:47.401
The Japanese have even elevated paper-folding to an art form known as "origami." People began to use paper for other decorative purposes.

40
00:04:47.402 --> 00:04:53.813
For centuries, people in China, Korea, and Japan have been using paper to make windows and decorative screens.

41
00:04:53.814 --> 00:05:00.399
Using paper to create home furnishings is now also popular in many countries all over the world.

42
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.628
Paper has many practical uses as well. Paper money, for example, has had an enormous impact on society.

43
00:05:06.629 --> 00:05:12.635
Before its introduction, bartering and using coins made of precious metals were the norm.

44
00:05:12.636 --> 00:05:16.863
Paper money, on the other hand, is much easier to carry around and store.

45
00:05:16.864 --> 00:05:23.687
It also has another advantage; it merely represents the value of the wealth, so it is easily replaceable.

46
00:05:23.688 --> 00:05:27.687
Paper money led to the creation of today's credit card–based economies.

47
00:05:27.688 --> 00:05:34.443
The plastic credit cards that move vast amounts of money would not have been possible without the introduction of paper money first.

48
00:05:34.444 --> 00:05:43.438
Packaging is another area in which paper has become indispensable, particularly in the manufacture of disposable paper products, such as paper cups and plates.

49
00:05:43.439 --> 00:05:48.017
But the availability of paper goods has led to overconsumption of paper products.

50
00:05:48.018 --> 00:05:58.411
For instance, North Americans now consume approximately three hundred kilograms of paper per person each year, compared to the world average of about fifty kilograms per person.

51
00:05:58.412 --> 00:06:06.333
This is another way that paper has changed our lives—the demand for large quantities of paper-based products is now causing environmental damage.

52
00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:19.739
A proliferation of paper-based advertisements, office documents, and junk mail has resulted in ever-increasing amounts of paper being thrown away, even while trees are continually being cut, leading to deforestation and global warming.

53
00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.899
On average, one tree produces about eighty thousand sheets of paper.

54
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:29.564
To supply the world's paper needs for one year, about 786 million trees are cut down.

55
00:06:29.565 --> 00:06:36.222
When paper was invented almost two thousand years ago, its inventor could not have imagined the changes that it would bring.

56
00:06:36.223 --> 00:06:40.141
It is difficult to imagine a modern world without this valuable material.

57
00:06:40.142 --> 00:06:45.386
It is no wonder that the Chinese consider paper to be one of their culture's foremost inventions.

58
00:06:45.387 --> 00:06:49.712
Indeed, it is one of the foremost inventions of human history.

